---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import { operator, profileHref } from '../lib/reviewers';
import { VALUES_POSITIONS } from '../lib/valuesPositions';

const op = operator();
// Headings and ids come from the list the Responsible AI matrix links into.
const [safety, environment, power, informed, human] = VALUES_POSITIONS;
---
<Base title="Values" description="Dangerous Robot's editorial position, plainly stated." layout="reading">
  <article class="reading">
    <Breadcrumb crumbs={[
      { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
      { label: 'Values' },
    ]} />
    <h1>Values</h1>

    <p>
      What Dangerous Robot believes. The research pages report what the
      sources show; our writing and petitions argue from the positions below.
    </p>

    <h2 id={safety.id}>{safety.heading}</h2>
    <p>
      Frontier AI is being deployed faster than the field can verify it is
      safe. Many people, including some who build these systems, consider
      human extinction a real possibility. A risk like climate change, but
      less measurable and more immediate.
    </p>
    <p>
      Dangerous Robot treats every vendor safety claim as a claim, not a fact.
    </p>

    <h2 id={environment.id}>{environment.heading}</h2>
    <p>
      Frontier AI models burn through water and power at an industrial scale.
      Firms are rushing to build fossil fuel-powered data centers to protect
      market positions. Some tech companies are walking back their previous
      environmental commitments. Accelerating in the wrong direction.
    </p>

    <h2 id={power.id}>{power.heading}</h2>
    <p>
      A handful of companies, and the people who run them, decide what gets
      built, how fast it ships, and which risks are acceptable. Most of the
      people who will live with those choices have no say in them. This site
      exists to help more people have a say while they still can.
    </p>

    <h2 id={informed.id}>{informed.heading}</h2>
    <p>
      Choosing whether and how to use AI takes information only its makers
      have: what their products consume, who pays for them, and what happens
      to what you type. AI companies have a responsibility to make that
      information public, and to operate in support of the general welfare
      of humanity. Dangerous Robot reports what they publish and what they
      leave out.
    </p>

    <h2 id={human.id}>{human.heading}</h2>
    <p>
      AI is likely to change almost everything about how we work, learn, and
      relate to each other. We should be intentional about where it belongs in
      our lives and work, and keep human judgment where it matters. Listening
      to each other will matter more than ever.
    </p>

    <h2>Our contradictions</h2>
    <p>
      Dangerous Robot uses AI. We critique tools we depend on and burn
      electricity doing it. We aspire to be pragmatic and transparent.
    </p>
    <p>
      We try to be careful: small decisions go to small models, large
      models only when the task has value and demands it. Our research pipeline runs on Infomaniak,
      a Swiss provider using 100% renewable energy that <a href="https://news.infomaniak.com/en/infomaniak-inaugurates-a-revolutionary-data-center-that-recovers-100-of-its-energy-for-building-heating/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">recovers waste heat
      to warm buildings</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="signed"><a href={profileHref(op)}>{op.fullName}</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  article h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-2xl);
  }

  article > p:first-of-type {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .signed {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
  }
</style>
